{"availability":"available","full":"richtr/threeVR","name":"threeVR","description":"An orientation-aware Virtual Reality controller for web browsers built on top of three.js","category":"Other","language":"JavaScript","stars":439,"forks":95,"created":"2014-03-27T13:15:53Z","updated":"2017-04-08T01:26:22Z","branch":"master","homepage":"http://richtr.github.io/threeVR/examples/vr_basic.html","topics":[],"demo":"http://richtr.github.io/threeVR/examples/vr_basic.html","color":"#63362a","ink":"#f8c7a8","lastCheckedAt":"2026-10-08T00:28:32.737Z","lastAttemptAt":"2026-10-08T00:28:32.737Z","lastAvailableAt":"2026-10-08T00:28:32.737Z","metrics":[{"at":"2026-10-05T21:33:13.547Z","stars":439,"forks":95},{"at":"2026-10-08T00:28:32.737Z","stars":439,"forks":95}],"technologies":[],"overview":{"kind":"readme","version":1,"paragraphs":["####An orientation-aware Virtual Reality controller for three.js ####","threeVR is a virtual reality controller that makes it easy to build device-orientation aware applications on top of the three.js library.","threeVR listens for device orientation event changes and orients a three.js scene in the direction the user is facing. This library also provides manual user override controls so users can drag to look around a scene and pinch to zoom in on scene features. Once user interaction is complete, the threeVR library snaps the scene's camera back to the current device orientation position. threeVR also provides a set of custom event callbacks that web applications can use to build their own compelling user interfaces."],"features":[],"sourceUrl":"https://github.com/richtr/threeVR/blob/master/README.md","fingerprint":"24a3a342bb0681f09f6e4ba53d578bfd7a28012f0a0964b5d5c01009877a4d9c","extractedAt":"2026-10-05T21:33:13.665Z"},"overviewCheckedAt":"2026-10-05T21:33:13.665Z","projectInsights":{"version":1,"whatItDoes":{"text":"####An orientation-aware Virtual Reality controller for three.js ####","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/richtr/threeVR/blob/master